      { kind: "parity", index: 2, cap: "θ · twin column parity (twin CPM)", formula: "P[x,z] = ⊕_y D[x,y,z]  Q[x,z] = ⊕_y D[x,y,z+t_y]\nE[x,z] = P[x,z] ⊕ P[x−1,z+1]  F[x,z] = Q[x+1,z] ⊕ Q[x−2,z+36]\nD′[x,y,z] = D ⊕ E[x,z+26] ⊕ F[x,z+26+t_y]", p: "P[x] plain column parity", q: "Q[x] row-shifted column parity", note: "t_y = (41, 50, 6, 10, 27) appears in both Q and the final injection; this is the key to the \"symmetric\" construction, avoiding the degenerate transpose map of the asymmetric twin CPM. The document gives differential and linear branch numbers of 12 (Keccak's θ: 4), the basis of both analyses. About 6400 XOR2/round in hardware, twice Keccak's θ.", srcIndex: 1, srcLabel: "x−1 → x (some sources)", depNote: "Every lane is updated; highlighted column is an example", detail: "P is the plain column parity, Q the column parity with row rotation; E uses P[x], P[x−1], F uses Q[x+1], Q[x−2]; both are rotated again by y and added back into each lane." },
      { kind: "permute", samples: [[1,0],[0,2],[3,1],[2,4]], cap: "π · affine lane-coordinate reordering", formula: "(x, y) → (y, 2x + 3y + 2 mod 5)", note: "Differs from Keccak's π ((x,y) → (y, 2x+3y)) by a constant term +2. A pure position permutation leaving bits within lanes untouched: in software, variable renaming with no extra instructions; in hardware, wiring with no logic gates.", labelAllSources: true, map: function (x, y) { return [y,(2*x+3*y+2)%5]; } },
      { kind: "sbox", axis: "column", index: 2, width: 5, parallelNote: "× 64 z-slices × 5 columns = 320 5-bit S-boxes / round", cap: "ψ · cubic map along y", formula: "D′[x,y] = D[x,y+3] ⊕ ¬D[x,y+1] · D[x,y+2] · ¬D[x,y+4]", note: "Opposite direction to Keccak's χ (along x). Differential uniformity 14, linearity 24, degree 3 forward and inverse; Keccak's χ: 8, 16, 2, compensated by θ's branch number 12. The DDT is not row-flat ({2,4,6,8,14}); the designers cite this against rebound attacks. Each output bit needs only XOR, AND, NOT; fully bitsliceable." }
